# DECSCL's conformance levels and 8-bit controls (S7C1T, S8C1T), which the parser reads one-byte
# C1 controls in and the replies use. A raw byte is written \xNN in `feed`.

test DA1 names the VT500 family and its features by default
spec VT510 DA1 (the family 65, the feature list); xterm ctlseqs CSI c
known-fail: alacritty – DA1 is a fixed ?6c
size 10 4
feed \e[c
reply \e[?65;1;4;6;9;15;16;17;18;21;22;28;29;52c

test DECSCL 62 takes the terminal down to VT200
spec VT510 DECSCL: Ps1 = 62 is level 2 (VT200 family); xterm ctlseqs DA2: terminal type 1 is a VT220
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e[62;1"p\e[c\e[>c
reply~ \e[?62;1;4;6;9;22;52c\e[>1;*;0c

test DECSCL 61 is VT100 mode
spec VT510 DECSCL: Ps1 = 61 is level 1 (VT100 mode); xterm ctlseqs DA1/DA2 for a VT100
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e[61"p\e[c\e[>c
reply~ \e[?1;2c\e[>0;*;0c

test DECRQM is a VT300 feature
spec VT510 DECRQM: introduced with the VT300 family; esctest2 DECSCL_Level2DoesntSupportDECRQM
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e[62;1"p\e[4$p\e[?7$p
reply none
feed \e[63;1"p\e[4$p\e[?7$p
reply \e[4;2$y\e[?7;1$y

test left and right margins are a VT400 feature
spec VT510 DECLRMM and DECSLRM: introduced with the VT400 family
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e[63;1"p\e[?69h\e[3;5s\e[1;3Habcd
screen
|  abcd    |
feed \e[64;1"p\e[?69h\e[3;5s\e[1;3Habcd
screen
|  abc     |
|  d       |
cursor 2,4

test DECSCL is a hard reset
spec VT510 DECSCL: "the terminal does a hard reset when the conformance level changes"
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed x\e[4h\e[3;3H\e7\e[64;1"p
screen
|          |
cursor 1,1
feed \e[4$p
reply \e[4;2$y

test DECRQSS reports the level and the control mode
spec xterm ctlseqs: DECRQSS for DECSCL answers with the level (61..65) and 0 or 2 for 8-bit controls, 1 for 7-bit
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \eP$q"p\e\\
reply \eP1$r65;1"p\e\\
feed \e[65;0"p\eP$q"p\e\\
reply \x901$r65;0"p\x9c

test DECSCL without its second parameter chooses 8-bit controls
spec VT510 DECSCL: "CSI 64 \" p" is VT Level 4 mode with 8-bit controls; with 1 the controls are 7-bit, with 0 or 2 8-bit; VT100 mode has 7-bit controls only
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e[64"p\e[5n
reply \x9b0n
feed \e[64;1"p\e[5n
reply \e[0n
feed \e[64;2"p\e[5n
reply \x9b0n
feed \e[61"p\e[5n
reply \e[0n

test S8C1T makes the replies use one-byte C1 controls
spec VT510 S8C1T: the terminal sends C1 controls as single 8-bit characters; xterm ctlseqs ESC SP G
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e G\e[5n
reply \x9b0n
feed \e F\e[5n
reply \e[0n

test C1 bytes are controls only in 8-bit mode
spec ECMA-48 5.3: the 8-bit C1 characters 08/00 to 09/15 are ESC 04/00 to 05/15 in 7-bit codes; xterm ctlseqs: a C1 byte can be mistaken for badly-formed UTF-8
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed a\x9b2Gb
screen
|a\u{fffd}2Gb|
feed \e G\r\x9b2Gc\e F
screen
|ac2Gb    |

test 8-bit IND, NEL, RI, HTS, DECID, SPA and EPA
spec ECMA-48 8.3: IND 08/04, NEL 08/05, HTS 08/08, RI 08/13, SPA 09/06, EPA 09/07; VT510 DECID
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e G\e[2;3H\x84X\x85Y\x8dZ
screen
|          |
|          |
| ZX       |
|Y         |
cursor 3,3
feed \x9aD
reply~ \x9b?*c
feed \e F

test an 8-bit ST ends a string
spec ECMA-48 8.3.143: ST is 09/12
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e G\x9d2;title\x9c\e F
title title

test an 8-bit CSI breaks off a sequence
spec ECMA-48 5.3
known-fail: alacritty – no DECSCL, S7C1T or S8C1T: the conformance level and 8-bit controls do not exist
size 10 4
feed \e G\e[3\x9b2;2HX\e F
screen
|          |
| X        |

test DA1 lists what each family has
spec VT510 DA1 and xterm ctlseqs CSI c: the VT300 family adds the technical set, the locator and user windows (DECRQDE), the VT400 family terminal state reports, horizontal scrolling and rectangular editing
known-fail: alacritty – DA1 is a fixed ?6c
size 10 4
feed \e[63;1"p\e[c
reply \e[?63;1;4;6;9;15;16;18;22;29;52c
feed \e[64;1"p\e[c
reply \e[?64;1;4;6;9;15;16;17;18;21;22;28;29;52c
